{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-84b3e2b68bf3c771","type":"tool_result","content":"model-registry-operator.md:14:**Short**: Kubernetes operator that manages the lifecycle of ModelRegistry, Catalog, and AIHub custom resources, provisioning model-registry service instances with kube-rbac-proxy authentication, PostgreSQL storage, and Gateway API or OpenShift Route ingress. [source: cmd/modelregistry.go:169, internal/controller/modelregistry_controller.go:282, internal/controller/modelcatalog_controller.go:183]\nmodel-registry-operator.md:16:**Detailed**: The model-registry-operator is a controller-runtime-based Kubernetes operator that reconciles three CRDs — ModelRegistry (v1alpha1/v1beta1), Catalog (v1alpha1), and AIHub (v1alpha1) — to provision and manage model registry service instances within the RHOAI platform. For each ModelRegistry CR, the operator creates a Deployment running the model-registry proxy backed by either PostgreSQL or MySQL storage, with an optional kube-rbac-proxy sidecar that enforces SubjectAccessReview-based authorization on the HTTPS port. The operator manages the full resource lifecycle including Services, ServiceAccounts, RBAC Roles/Bindings, Secrets, NetworkPolicies, and ingress resources (Gateway API HTTPRoutes or OpenShift Routes depending on cluster capabilities). The ModelCatalog reconciler similarly provisions a centralized catalog service with its own PostgreSQL instance. The operator detects cluster capabilities at startup — including OpenShift APIs, BYOIDC mode (where user.openshift.io is absent), Gateway API availability, and the platform Auth CRD — to conditionally create platform-specific resources. [source: internal/controller/util.go:36-39, internal/controller/config/defaults.go:197-211, internal/controller/config/templates/deployment.yaml.tmpl:220-272]\nmodel-registry-operator.md:53:| model-catalog-postgres | Controller-created Deployment | postgresql ({image}) |\nmodel-registry-operator.md:57:| {registry-name}-postgres | Controller-created Deployment | postgres ({image}) |\nmodel-registry-operator.md:137:| model-catalog-postgres | ClusterIP | 5432/TCP | 5432 | PostgreSQL | Unknown | Unknown | Internal |\nmodel-registry-operator.md:141:| {registry-name}-postgres | ClusterIP | 5432/TCP | 5432 | PostgreSQL | Unknown | Unknown | Internal |\nmodel-registry-operator.md:246:| model-catalog-postgres | Opaque | model-catalog/catalog, model-catalog-postgres/postgresql | controller template | Unknown |\nmodel-registry-operator.md:248:| {registry-name}-postgres-credentials | Opaque | {registry-name}-postgres/postgres | controller Go source | Unknown |\nmodel-registry-operator.md:250:| {name}-postgres | Opaque |  | controller Go source | Unknown |\nmodel-registry-operator.md:302:- **Data storage:** Each model-registry instance connects to a PostgreSQL or MySQL database with configurable connection parameters including SSL/TLS certificates, keys, and root CA bundles mounted from Secrets and ConfigMaps. Database credentials are injected via environment variables from referenced Secrets. [source: internal/controller/config/templates/deployment.yaml.tmpl:40-68, 107-131]\nai4rag.md:16:**Detailed**: ai4rag provides an end-to-end RAG optimization toolkit that orchestrates document ingestion from S3-compatible storage, text chunking (via Docling and LangChain strategies), embedding generation, vector store indexing (Milvus, PGVector, or Chroma), retrieval, LLM-based answer generation, and evaluation. It uses a hyperparameter optimization loop based on Generalized Additive Models (GAM) to search over chunking parameters, embedding models, foundation models, and retrieval strategies, producing ranked RAG pattern configurations. The library connects to an OpenAI-compatible Model-as-a-Service (MaaS) endpoint for both LLM inference and embedding generation, and includes a KFP event handler for integration with Data Science Pipelines. It is packaged as a Python library with no HTTP server or Kubernetes deployment manifests of its own. [source: pyproject.toml:6-7, 30-44, ai4rag/components/utils/maas_client.py:30-62, ai4rag/utils/event_handler/event_handler.py:47-55, ai4rag/core/hpo/gam_opt.py:1]\nai4rag.md:24:The vector store layer supports three pluggable backends — Milvus (with gRPC TLS and PEM certificate materialization), PGVector (PostgreSQL with connection pooling), and Chroma (ephemeral, persistent, or client/server modes) — all configured via environment variables. Credentials for S3 storage, vector stores, and the MaaS endpoint are injected through environment variables, with no Kubernetes Secret manifests defined by the component itself. [source: ai4rag/rag/vector_store/config.py:1-258, ai4rag/components/utils/s3.py:10-31]\nai4rag.md:94:| pgvector | ~=0.5.0 | Yes | Unknown | Python package dependency |\nai4rag.md:150:| PGVECTOR_PASSWORD | environment variable | Python application | runtime environment | Unknown |\nai4rag.md:188:- **Embedding and indexing:** Chunked text is sent to an OpenAI-compatible MaaS endpoint for embedding generation, then indexed in the configured vector store (Milvus via gRPC with optional TLS, PGVector via PostgreSQL wire protocol, or Chroma via HTTP). [source: ai4rag/rag/embedding/openai_model.py:31-36, ai4rag/rag/vector_store/config.py:108-177, 181-249]\nai4rag.md:190:- **Security context:** 3 secret references (AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, MILVUS_TOKEN, PGVECTOR_PASSWORD) provide credential inputs for external service connections. All credentials are sourced from environment variables; no Kubernetes Secret manifests are defined by the component. [source: ai4rag/components/utils/s3.py:10, ai4rag/rag/vector_store/config.py:175, 248]\nogx-distribution.md:16:**Detailed**: ogx-distribution packages the OGX framework as a containerized Python service for RHOAI. It acts as a unified AI agent gateway that routes inference requests to configurable remote providers (vLLM, AWS Bedrock, IBM WatsonX, Azure, Vertex AI, OpenAI, Gemini, Anthropic), provides vector I/O through Milvus, PGVector, or Qdrant backends, and offers tool runtime capabilities including web search and file operations. The distribution uses PostgreSQL for both key-value and SQL-backed persistence of agent state, conversations, batches, and metadata. Authentication is handled via OAuth2 token validation with JWKS when configured. The container image is built on an AIPCC base image with Python dependencies installed from the RHEL AI Python Package Index via Hermeto-prefetched packages, and ships pre-fetched Docling and embedding model artifacts. [source: distribution/config.yaml:1-280, Dockerfile.konflux:1-58, distribution/entrypoint.sh:1-79]\nogx-distribution.md:26:Storage is centralized on PostgreSQL with two backend types: `kv_postgres` for key-value stores (registry metadata, agent state, vector store namespaces) and `sql_postgres` for relational data (inference logs, conversations, batches, file metadata, connectors). This dual-backend pattern allows the framework to use the appropriate access pattern for each data type while sharing a single database instance. [source: distribution/config.yaml:198-235]\nogx-distribution.md:90:| PostgreSQL | SQL/KV client | Required | Key-value and relational storage for agent state, conversations, batches, metadata, inference logs, and vector store metadata |\nogx-distribution.md:160:| 3 | OGX Server | PostgreSQL | 5432/TCP | PostgreSQL | Platform-delegated | Username/password |\nogx-distribution.md:167:| 2 | OGX Server | Vector DB (Milvus / PGVector / Qdrant) | Configurable | HTTP/gRPC/SQL | Configurable (mTLS for Milvus) | Token / API key / password |\nogx-distribution.md:169:| 4 | OGX Server | PostgreSQL | 5432/TCP | PostgreSQL | Platform-delegated | Username/password |\nogx-distribution.md:177:| PostgreSQL | SQL/KV client | Required | 5432/TCP | PostgreSQL | Configurable (platform-delegated) | Persistent storage for all state, metadata, and relational data |