H-C-E003 is the documented deviation (manual setup) |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with no loss of capability | E002 (provider-level validation), all three branch epics (end-to-end) |\n| HLR-3 | P0 | Vector store runs as separate process, decoupled from OGX instance | E002, all branch epics |\n| HLR-4 | P0 | Multi-tenant scoping (per-namespace isolation) | BRANCH-A-E003, BRANCH-B-E003 |\n| HLR-5 | P1 | Lightweight for resource-constrained environments | E002 (sizing input from benchmark), BRANCH-A/B |\n\n(Auto Rag AC removed per Staff override #3 — covered by RHAISTRAT-7824.)\n\nEvery HLR is covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- Missing strategy attachment: Affected Components / Impacted Teams / HLR / Dependencies / NFR tables unavailable; reconstruction noted above; teams inferred.\n- Priority inversion risk (inherent to the fallback design): BRANCH-C-E003 is P1 as a contingency, but if the go/no-go selects it, it becomes the release-blocking path and should be re-prioritized to P0 at selection time. RHAISTRAT-7474 (inline provider removal) is blocked on an out-of-the-box replacement; selecting branch C leaves that blocker only partially resolved.\n- Architecture-context nuance: the ogx-distribution context file (rhoai-3.6-ea.1) describes PostgreSQL-centralized application storage and already-stripped inline providers, while Staff Engineer Input corrects that OGX defaults to SQLite and the 3.5 distribution still ships the inline Milvus provider. Staff input takes precedence; per Correction 3 the application storage backend is untouched, so the discrepancy does not affect epic structure.\n- External schedule risk: RHAISTRAT-7164 (In Progress, another strategy) is a hard precondition for the preferred branch; its timing is not controlled here — captured as the go/no-go gate rather than a DAG edge to a nonexistent epic.\n- No docs-authoring epic: the only documentation in scope (branch C setup documentation, d