{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-bf369956-35be-4245-a545-f98ade9dff41","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a75975af8574f848","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-xqjz7889/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8004: Default Vector Store for Gen AI Studio\n\n## Context and Input Fidelity\n\nThe full strategy body is stored as a Jira attachment (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`) that is not present in the pre-provisioned workspace. The Affected Components table, Impacted Teams table, HLR list, Dependencies table, and NFRs were therefore reconstructed from the strategy Summary, Acceptance Criteria, and the Staff Engineer Input (which takes precedence). HLR ids below refer to the acceptance-criteria-derived requirements listed in the HLR Traceability Matrix.\n\nTeams: the Impacted Teams table was unavailable; teams are inferred from component ownership — Gen AI Studio for the odh-dashboard gen-ai BFF / playground work, OGX for the ogx-distribution work. Re-assign at submit time if the canonical team names differ.\n\nComponent mappings (canonical names from `.context/rhai-components.txt`):\n- ogx-distribution repository work (pgvector vector_io provider) → `OGX Core`\n- odh-dashboard gen-ai BFF / playground provisioning work → `Gen AI Studio`\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n\nThe Staff Engineer Input diverges from the AI-generated technical approach in the (unavailable) strategy body; the following overrides were applied:\n\n1. **No image onboarding chain.** The Red Hat `postgresql-16` image already includes pgvector, the model-registry-operator already auto-provisions it, and `RELATED_IMAGE_POSTGRESQL_16_IMAGE` is already in the rhods-operator CSV. No custom image build, no Konflux onboarding, no repo onboarding — the strategy's 1-2 sprint image-onboarding estimate is eliminated.\n2. **Application storage untouched.** Only the OGX vector_io provider is configured for pgvector (`remote::pgvector`); OGX application storage stays on its existing SQLite default. No SQLite-to-PostgreSQL migration and no vector/application storage consolidation work.\n3. **Auto Rag decoupled.** Removed from scope; pgvector is validated only for Gen AI Studio playground RAG. Auto Rag compatibility is RHAISTRAT-7824's scope. The strategy AC \"Auto Rag functions with the default vector store with no loss of current capability\" is consequently out of scope for this decomposition.\n4. **Platform-provided shared PostgreSQL via DSC arrives in RHOAI 3.6.** For 3.5, Gen AI Studio must provision its own PostgreSQL (or use a fallback); portability to the shared connection string is a design constraint, not a 3.5 deliverable.\n5. **Hard operator dependency.** Operator-based self-provisioning requires the RHOAI-controlled operator delivered by RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization, In Progress). The upstream ogx-k8s-operator cannot carry RHOAI-specific provisioning logic.\n6. **Go/no-go gate.** The development team must define a go/no-go gate in sprint planning selecting one of three bounded provisioning outcomes: (A) operator-based self-provisioning, (B) gen-ai BFF-managed provisioning, (C) customer-supplied (bring-your-own) PostgreSQL.\n7. **Performance scope.** Dev-experimentation only: capture the inline Milvus baseline and verify pgvector is within the same order of magnitude; flat scan (no index) is sufficient; no latency SLAs. Latency-threshold, corpus-size, and index-type open questions are removed.\n8. **Resource sizing.** Start from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL requests/limits; adjust per benchmark.\n9. **Provider integration is minor.** The existing remote vector store implementation is largely reusable; the majority of effort is PostgreSQL/pgvector infrastructure and provisioning.\n10. **Scoping model resolved.** Per-namespace isolation is preferred (the per-instance need belonged to Auto Rag, now decoupled).\n11. **Migration path resolved directionally.** The default store targets development experimentation; production-scale vector workloads should use an external vector database. A configuration path to an external vector DB is in scope; a data-migration epic is not.\n\n## Epic List\n\nMain plan:\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Go/no-go decision: default vector store provisioning approach for RHOAI 3.5 | Investigation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Enable and validate the pgvector remote vector_io provider in the OGX distribution | Implementation | OGX | P0 |\n\nBranch epics (conditional alternatives, attached to E001's outcome — not standalone Jira issues):\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E003 | Operator-based PostgreSQL/pgvector self-provisioning for Gen AI Studio playgrounds | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003 | Gen AI Studio BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E003 | Bring-your-own PostgreSQL configuration for playground RAG | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 (Investigation): provisioning go/no-go gate\"]\n    E002[\"E002 (Implementation): pgvector vector_io provider validation - OGX Core\"]\n    BA[\"BRANCH-A-E003: operator-based self-provisioning\"]\n    BB[\"BRANCH-B-E003: BFF-managed provisioning\"]\n    BC[\"BRANCH-C-E003: bring-your-own PostgreSQL\"]\n    E001 --> BA\n    E001 --> BB\n    E001 --> BC\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → BRANCH-A-E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The go/no-go outcome determines whether the operator-based epic exists (true gate, `remove`: if the dashboard operator modularization does not land for 3.5, this path is not built). Bounded outcome (3 possibilities) → conditional branch. |\n| E001 → BRANCH-B-E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | Same gate; the BFF-managed path exists only if selected as the fallback when the operator path is unavailable. True gate, `remove`. |\n| E001 → BRANCH-C-E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | Same gate; the bring-your-own path exists only if selected. True gate, `remove`. |\n| E002 — no incoming/outgoing edges | Step 5.5 (Tier 1) | Provider enablement, validation, and the Milvus-baseline benchmark are independent of the provisioning decision — risk-reduction work that proceeds regardless of which branch is selected, and the sprint-1 benchmark explicitly precedes the decision. Runs in parallel with E001 and with any branch. |\n| Branch epics ↛ dependency on E002 | Rules 13/14 | The provider validation produces no build-against artifact (API, CRD, or library) for the provisioning work — the pgvector provider already exists in the distribution. End-to-end playground validation is an acceptance criterion on each branch epic, not a DAG edge. |\n| No edge to RHAISTRAT-7164 | Rule 21 / external dependency | RHAISTRAT-7164 is a separate strategy (In Progress), not an epic in this decomposition; it is modeled as the precondition evaluated by the E001 go/no-go gate, with tiered delivery via the branch epics. |\n\n## Branch Plan (conditional decomposition)\n\nE001 has three bounded outcomes that change which downstream epics exist:\n\n- **Branch A — operator-based self-provisioning** (preferred; requires RHAISTRAT-7164 to land for 3.5): PostgreSQL lifecycle management in the RHOAI-controlled operator, following the model-registry-operator pattern.\n- **Branch B — BFF-managed provisioning**: the gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL Deployment/Service/PVC/Secret when a playground with RAG is configured (as it does for guardrails infrastructure), with explicit lifecycle design (cleanup, pod restarts, credential rotation) and portability to the 3.6 shared platform database.\n- **Branch C — bring-your-own PostgreSQL**: users configure their own PostgreSQL connection details; documented deviation from the out-of-the-box requirement.\n\nEach branch epic sets `branch`, `gated_by=RHAISTRAT-8004-E001` (also in `dependencies`), and `gate_failure_impact.action=remove` — unselected branches are not created as Jira issues. Exactly one branch is selected by the gate.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\nHLRs derived from the strategy's Acceptance Criteria (the strategy's own HLR table is in the unavailable attachment), after Staff overrides:\n\n| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|-------------|---------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | Default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning of external infrastructure | E001 (selects mechanism), BRANCH-A-E003, BRANCH-B-E003 — BRANCH-C-E003 is the documented deviation (manual setup by design) |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store, no loss of current capability | E002 (provider-level validation), BRANCH-A/B/C-E003 (end-to-end) |\n| HLR-3 | P0 | Vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX/Llama Stack instance (not inline) | E002, BRANCH-A/B/C-E003 |\n| HLR-4 | P0 | Scoped for multi-tenant environments (per-namespace isolation) | BRANCH-A-E003, BRANCH-B-E003 |\n| HLR-5 | P1 | Lightweight for resource-constrained environments | E002 (benchmark-informed sizing input), BRANCH-A-E003, BRANCH-B-E003 |\n\nCoverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements. The strategy's Auto Rag acceptance criterion is removed per Staff override 3 (tracked in RHAISTRAT-7824). No lower-priority HLR is bundled into a higher-priority epic — each branch epic is a single conditional alternative covering the HLRs its outcome can satisfy.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy attachment (input fidelity):** the Affected Components, Impacted Teams, HLR, Dependencies, and NFR tables live in `RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`, which is not in the workspace. Scope was reconstructed from the Summary, Acceptance Criteria, and Staff Engineer Input. Teams are inferred from component ownership — verify before submit.\n- **Priority inversion risk inherent to the fallback design:** BRANCH-C-E003 is P1 as a contingency, but if the go/no-go gate selects it, it becomes the release-blocking path (RHAISTRAT-7474 remains blocked on an out-of-the-box replacement) and must be re-prioritized to P0 at selection time.\n- **Architecture-context nuance:** the ogx-distribution context file (rhoai-3.6-ea.1) describes PostgreSQL-centralized application storage and inline providers already stripped, while Staff Engineer Input corrects that OGX defaults to SQLite for application storage and that the 3.5-era distribution still ships the inline Milvus provider. Staff input takes precedence; per Correction 3 the application storage backend is untouched, so the discrepancy does not change epic structure — E002's scope is the vector_io provider only.\n- **External schedule risk (not an AC):** RHAISTRAT-7164 (In Progress) is a hard precondition for the preferred branch and its timing is outside this strategy's control. It is captured as the E001 go/no-go gate rather than a DAG edge to a nonexistent epic, and as a risk on BRANCH-A-E003.\n- **No docs-authoring epic:** the only documentation in scope (branch C setup documentation, the E001 decision record) is incidental to implementation work, not substantive content authoring; the strategy does not declare Documentation Support.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\n- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately):** E002 — pgvector provider enablement/validation and the inline-Milvus-baseline benchmark. Delivers risk reduction and the sizing input that every branch consumes, with no dependency on the provisioning decision.\n- **Tier 2 (gated on E001's outcome):** the provisioning