{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-dd6cb000-bdac-465e-a465-26f77b18e770","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-86b0fb04adb6ac7c","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-xqjz7889/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E002.md","content":"# Enable and Validate the pgvector Remote Vector_io Provider in the OGX Distribution\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic makes the pgvector remote vector_io provider a validated, working option for Gen AI Studio playground RAG in the OGX distribution, and produces the benchmark and sizing evidence that any provisioning approach consumes. It is common to all provisioning outcomes: the provider integration itself is minor because the existing remote vector store implementation is largely reusable (per the engineering team), and the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image already includes pgvector — no custom image build, no Konflux onboarding, and no repo onboarding are needed. This work is Tier 1: it starts immediately and runs in parallel with the provisioning go/no-go decision, because the sprint-1 benchmark deliberately precedes that decision.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Confirm the pgvector remote vector_io provider (`remote::pgvector`, enabled via the distribution's provider environment-variable guard) is available and functional in the OGX distribution for Gen AI Studio playground RAG workloads, reusing the existing remote vector store implementation\n- Capture the inline Milvus baseline benchmark and verify pgvector performance is within the same order of magnitude for typical playground RAG document sets — development-experimentation scope, no latency SLAs (p50/p95/p99 thresholds are explicitly not required)\n- Confirm flat scan (no vector index) is sufficient for the default store's typical document-set sizes; users with production-scale vector workloads are directed to an external vector database\n- Produce a PostgreSQL resource sizing recommendation using the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL resource requests/limits as the starting point, adjusted by the benchmark results\n- Validate that the vector store operates as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance (a remote provider, not an inline provider embedded in the OGX process)\n- Scope excludes OGX application storage: the key-value and SQL storage backends remain on their existing default and are not migrated or consolidated\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Vector insert and query operations through the OGX vector_io API succeed against a pgvector-backed remote PostgreSQL store\n- A benchmark report captures the inline Milvus baseline and demonstrates pgvector within the same order of magnitude for typical playground document sets\n- The default store configuration uses flat scan where the benchmark shows it sufficient for typical document sets, and the report states the corpus-size boundary beyond which an external vector database is recommended\n- A resource sizing recommendation for the pgvector PostgreSQL instance is documented, referencing the model-registry-operator's requests/limits and the benchmark evidence\n- The validated configuration runs the vector store as a separate process from the OGX instance, with no inline vector provider in the serving path\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-2 (P0 — playground RAG workflows function with no loss of current capability): provider-level validation of vector insert/query/retrieval parity ahead of the end-to-end workflow validation carried by the provisioning work\n- HLR-3 (P0 — vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance): remote-provider validation\n- HLR-5 (P1 — lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments): benchmark-informed sizing recommendation consumed by the provisioning work\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":72822,"output_tokens":0},"context_manage