{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a423c8634ab4d839","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n3\ttitle: 'Go/no-go decision: default vector store provisioning approach for RHOAI 3.5'\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n6\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 0\n13\t  local_runnability: 0\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: 0\n15\t  human_judgment_required: -2\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Low\n19\tai_implementability_score: -1\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# Go/No-Go Decision: Default Vector Store Provisioning Approach for RHOAI 3.5\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tThis epic resolves the central unknown of the strategy: how the default pgvector-backed vector store is provisioned for Gen AI Studio playground RAG in RHOAI 3.5. It produces a decision record that selects exactly one of three bounded outcomes — (A) operator-based PostgreSQL self-provisioning in the RHOAI-controlled operator delivered by the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164), (B) gen-ai BFF-managed provisioning, or (C) customer-supplied (bring-your-own) PostgreSQL. The selection is gated primarily on whether RHAISTRAT-7164 lands in time for the 3.5 release, and determines which downstream provisioning implementation is built. The Staff Engineer Input directs the development team to define this go/no-go gate as part of sprint planning.\n30\t\n31\t## Scope\n32\t\n33\t- Define the go/no-go gate with explicit trigger criteria: delivery status of the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164), RHOAI 3.5 release-timeline fit, and lifecycle-management risk of each option\n34\t- Assess whether the operator layer delivered by the dashboard operator modularization can carry PostgreSQL lifecycle management following the model-registry-operator's auto-provisioning pattern in the available time\n35\t- Weigh the BFF-managed fallback (the gen-ai BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop; cleanup on playground deletion, pod-restart handling, and credential rotation need explicit design attention) against requiring customers to bring their own PostgreSQL\n36\t- Produce a decision record selecting exactly one outcome, with the evidence evaluated and the conditional plan for the selected approach\n37\t- If a fallback is selected, document the deviation from the out-of-the-box requirement and the impact on the inline-provider removal work that this strategy unblocks\n38\t\n39\t## Acceptance Criteria\n40\t\n41\t- A decision record exists that selects exactly one of the three provisioning approaches for RHOAI 3.5\n42\t- The record states the go/no-go criteria evaluated (dashboard operator modularization delivery status, release timing, lifecycle-management risk) and the evidence used for each\n43\t- The go/no-go gate is defined, executed, and its outcome communicated as part of sprint planning\n44\t- If the operator-based path is not selected, the record names the selected fallback and documents the deviation from the out-of-the-box, no-manual-setup requirement\n45\t\n46\t## HLR Traceability\n47\t\n48\t- HLR-1 (P0 — default vector store available out-of-the-box): this decision determines the mechanism by which the out-of-the-box store is delivered, and whether the requirement is met directly or via a documented fallback\n49\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-907d5e1a60a63443","agent_id":"a0e8e02b2ce07fa25","session_id":"02e875f2-6cfe-4933-bc28-36b9ef9ef3bb","uuid":"67a10c3d-1b31-495b-ae22-3b9cbfcf3c74","timestamp":"2026-10-26T20:44:45.973Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a423c8634ab4d839","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
