{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-adba71b8a47f67f5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8004\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: HLR Coverage\n9\t  description: HLR-4 and HLR-5 rows in the HLR Traceability Matrix omit BRANCH-C-E003\n10\t    without the explanatory note that HLR-1 row carries; under bring-your-own infrastructure,\n11\t    per-namespace isolation is satisfied by construction and footprint is outside\n12\t    platform control — record that rationale in those matrix rows.\n13\terror: null\n14\t---\n15\t## Review Summary\n16\t\n17\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n18\tRecommendation: accept\n19\t\n20\tContract validator: OK (body sections, canonical component names, priority inheritance, and branch metadata all pass).\n21\t\n22\tTriage check: `triage` is null (full decomposition); the 5-epic output (2 main-plan + 3 conditional branches) is the expected shape. No triage contradiction.\n23\t\n24\tInput fidelity note: the strategy body lives in a Jira attachment absent from the workspace; the decomposer reconstructed the HLR list from the Acceptance Criteria and Staff Engineer Input, and documented this limitation (plus inferred teams) in Health Warnings rather than silently guessing — acceptable handling of a missing input.\n25\t\n26\t## Criterion Details\n27\t\n28\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n29\tAll five HLRs derived from the strategy's Acceptance Criteria map to at least one epic, and every mapping was verified against the epics' HLR Traceability sections: HLR-1 (E001, BRANCH-A/B-E003, with BRANCH-C-E003 explicitly recorded as the documented deviation), HLR-2/3 (E002 plus all branches), HLR-4 (BRANCH-A/B-E003), HLR-5 (E002, BRANCH-A/B-E003). The Auto Rag acceptance criterion is removed per Staff Engineer Input (\"Remove Auto Rag from ... acceptance criteria\"), documented as override 3 and tracked to RHAISTRAT-7824 — not a silent drop. Priority inheritance is correct: E001 (P0) gates all branch work; BRANCH-C-E003 is P1 but blocks nothing (it is one of three mutually exclusive alternatives), and the summary's Health Warnings explicitly records that it must be re-prioritized to P0 if selected — the inversion risk is on the record rather than hidden. No priority collapse: HLR-5 (P1) shares the implementation surface with the P0 work in each epic (sizing of the same provisioned instance), so no deferrable feature is bundled. Minor observation (see issues): the HLR-4 and HLR-5 matrix rows omit BRANCH-C-E003 without the explanatory note that HLR-1's row carries; isolation and sizing are satisfied by construction (or out of platform control) under user-supplied infrastructure, but the row should say so.\n30\t\n31\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n32\tNo circular dependencies. All three edges (E001 → BRANCH-A/B/C-E003) reference existing branch files, match the branch epics' frontmatter `dependencies`, and the diagram uses the correct dependency→dependent arrow convention. All three branch files set both `branch` (matching filename letters) and `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001`, and each `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies` list. No main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its `dependencies` — E002 correctly depends on nothing, and the branch conditionality hangs off the Investigation itself, exactly as the rules require. Cross-check of epic content for uncaptured data dependencies: the branch epics state they consume E002's provider validation, benchmark, and sizing recommendation; the decomposer addressed this explicitly in the DAG Justification (no build-against artifact; end-to-end validation and the sizing envelope are ACs on each branch epic), which is the rule-compliant AC-gated coupling shape for a configuration/recommendation input rather than a `dependencies` edge — accepted, and per the review rules its absence from the DAG is not reported as a missing edge. RHAISTRAT-7164 (external, In Progress strategy) is correctly modeled as the E001 gate precondition rather than an edge to a nonexistent epic. No konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism is vacuously satisfied. Critical path length 2 (E001 → selected branch) is appropriate: Staff corrections eliminated the image-onboarding chain, reducing the effective strategy to a small gate-plus-two-workstream shape (S: 1-2).\n33\t\n34\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n35\tComponent/team tuples are consistent and separate: E002 is OGX Core/OGX; E001 and all branches are Gen AI Studio/Gen AI Studio. Both component names are in the canonical list (validator-confirmed). No epic spans multiple components or teams. Each branch epic is a single logical workstream (one provisioning mechanism) within the ~2-week guideline; E002 bundles provider validation, benchmark, and sizing, but these are one logical change (validate the provider, produce the evidence) seeded by an existing implementation, not heterogeneous work streams. The three branch epics cover the same HLRs but are mutually exclusive conditional alternatives gated to exactly one selection — this is the intended conditional-branch shape, not scope duplication. No work-product type mixing: BRANCH-C's setup documentation is incidental to the implementation (the summary's Health Warnings records the no-docs-authoring-epic reasoning), not substantive content authoring.\n36\t\n37\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n38\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (which of three bounded provisioning approaches) determines which downstream epics exist — the definition of a true gate, and the Staff Engineer Input explicitly assigns this go/no-go to sprint planning. E002 is a correct Implementation producing concrete artifacts (validated provider configuration, benchmark report, sizing recommendation). All three branch epics are Implementation epics producing concrete deliverables. Gating metadata is complete and correct on every branch epic: `gated_by` set, `gate_failure_impact` populated with both `action: remove` and a meaningful `fallback_approach` describing what happens if that branch is not selected. No epic lists an Investigation dependency without `gated_by`, and no `gated_by` reference is only transitively reachable.\n39\t\n40\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n41\tType-appropriate signal fields are used everywhere: E001 carries `investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric), all four Implementation epics carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric), and each rationale file's values were cross-checked against the frontmatter — all match. E001's signals are well-calibrated: `question_specificity: 1` (three enumerable outcomes with named criteria), `source_accessibility: 0` (the gating input — RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery status — does not yet exist in any readable artifact; correctly not scored 1 despite readable supporting material), `human_judgment_required: -2` (the Staff input explicitly assigns the call to the team's sprint planning). Implementation signals are justified against strategy content: BRANCH-A's `external_dependency: -1` (hard RHAISTRAT-7164 dependency) and `existing_foundation: 0`, BRANCH-B's `open_questions: -1` (unresolved lifecycle design the Staff input flags), and BRANCH-C's `adapter_pattern: 0` are all defensible rubric applications. `architecture_claims` calibration verified against `.context`: all four Implementation epics score 1 with rationales citing the actual context files (ogx-distribution.md for the PGVector backend and remote-provider wiring, model-registry-operator.md for the auto-provisioning precedent, odh-dashboard.md for the gen-ai BFF, ogx-k8s-operator.md for the OGXServer CR surface) — the strategy makes architecture claims and the citations ground them. Computed aggregates were not checked per review rules.\n42\t\n43\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n44\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated AC inventory: no replacement epics exist (the inline-provider removal is RHAISTRAT-7474's scope; this strategy adds the replacement store), no docs-authoring epic exists (the strategy declares no Documentation Support requirement), and no konflux chain exists (Staff Correction 2 eliminated image onboarding) — so no rule-mandated AC is applicable and missing. The branch epics carry the end-to-end playground RAG validation as ACs, the correct coupling to E002's outputs. Soft-AC scan: no AC states an externally-controlled precondition — RHAISTRAT-7164 timing is correctly kept out of the ACs and recorded as a gate/risk, and E001's sprint-planning AC is about the epic's own deliverable (the decision record). No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID; dependencies are described in prose (\"the OGX distribution work\") and via full epic IDs in frontmatter only.\n45\t\n46\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n47\tAll in-scope strategy ACs are accounted for: out-of-the-box availability (HLR-1 via branches, deviation path documented), playground RAG function (HLR-2, provider-level in E002 and end-to-end in branches), separate-process decoupling (HLR-3), multi-tenant scoping (HLR-4), lightweight footprint (HLR-5); the Auto Rag AC is removed per the explicit Staff override and tracked to RHAISTRAT-7824. Both strategy Open Questions are resolved and recorded as overrides 10 and 11 (per-namespace scoping; directional migration answer with the external-DB configuration path in every branch's scope). Staff Engineer Input items are all carried forward: resource sizing reference (E002 + branches), 3.6 shared-database portability as a design constraint with ACs (BRANCH-A/B), BFF lifecycle caveats as scope and ACs (BRANCH-B), fallback gating as E001's branch plan. The conditional branches cover all three bounded outcomes named in the Staff input (operator-based, BFF-managed, bring-your-own) with exactly-one-selected semantics. Strategy-level risks (RHAISTRAT-7164 timing, input-fidelity gap, priority inversion) are carried in Health Warnings rather than dropped. No docs-authoring epic is required — the strategy has no Prerequisites & Process Gates section declaring Documentation Support.\n48\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9073032f06d45f45","agent_id":"ab680fe0d56628d78","session_id":"02e875f2-6cfe-4933-bc28-36b9ef9ef3bb","uuid":"e6f90e5d-71ce-4c6f-80ba-3138836b9492","timestamp":"2026-10-26T20:47:50.130Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-adba71b8a47f67f5","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
