{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8fb7b0111f4f5c4d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E003\n3\ttitle: Gen AI Studio BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n5\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n6\tteam: Gen AI Studio\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tbranch: B\n10\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n11\tdependencies:\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: remove\n15\t  fallback_approach: If the go/no-go gate does not select the BFF-managed path, this\n16\t    epic is not built; provisioning is delivered by the operator-based path or reduced\n17\t    to the bring-your-own branch.\n18\tai_signals:\n19\t  change_specificity: 1\n20\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n21\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n22\t  existing_foundation: 1\n23\t  open_questions: -1\n24\t  external_dependency: 1\n25\t  human_process_gates: 0\n26\t  repo_access: 1\n27\t  architecture_claims: 1\n28\timplementation_type: null\n29\tinvestigation_signals: null\n30\tai_implementability: High\n31\tai_implementability_score: 6\n32\tjira_key: null\n33\t---\n34\t# Gen AI Studio BFF-Managed PostgreSQL/pgvector Provisioning\n35\t\n36\t## Description\n37\t\n38\tSelected if the provisioning go/no-go decision chooses the BFF-managed fallback — i.e., the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) does not land in time for RHOAI 3.5 and the team prefers this over requiring customers to bring their own PostgreSQL. This epic has the gen-ai BFF provision the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable instance exists in the namespace, similar to how the BFF currently provisions guardrails infrastructure. It uses the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image with pgvector (no custom image build, no Konflux onboarding). The known caveat: the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, so lifecycle management — cleanup on playground deletion, handling of pod restarts, credential rotation — needs explicit design attention.\n39\t\n40\t## Description of Dependencies\n41\t\n42\tThis epic cannot start until the provisioning go/no-go decision selects the BFF-managed outcome. It consumes the pgvector provider validation, benchmark, and sizing recommendation produced by the OGX distribution work as inputs to its configuration and sizing decisions.\n43\t\n44\t## Scope\n45\t\n46\t- Implement BFF-managed creation of the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance (Deployment, Service, persistent storage claim, credentials Secret) when a playground with RAG is configured and none exists in the namespace\n47\t- Wire the vector_io configuration of OGX instances created by the BFF for playgrounds to the provisioned pgvector store, scoped per namespace for multi-tenant isolation\n48\t- Design lifecycle management explicitly, since the BFF has no reconciliation loop: cleanup on playground deletion, recovery from PostgreSQL pod restarts, and credential rotation\n49\t- Design for portability: when the platform-provided shared PostgreSQL managed via the DSC becomes available (RHOAI 3.6), the BFF must consume the shared connection string rather than self-provisioning\n50\t- Apply resource requests/limits starting from the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL reference sizing, adjusted per the benchmark results\n51\t- Provide a configuration path to point playground RAG at an external, production-grade vector database for users whose workloads outgrow the default store\n52\t\n53\t## Acceptance Criteria\n54\t\n55\t- Configuring a Gen AI Studio playground with RAG causes the BFF to provision a pgvector-backed PostgreSQL instance in the namespace automatically, with no manual user setup of external infrastructure\n56\t- Provisioned vector stores are isolated per namespace; one tenant's playground cannot access another namespace's vector data\n57\t- End-to-end Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows (document upload, embedding, retrieval, grounded generation) function with the provisioned pgvector store with no loss of current capability compared to the inline provider\n58\t- Deleting a playground triggers cleanup of the BFF-provisioned PostgreSQL resources, and a PostgreSQL pod restart does not leave playground RAG in a broken state; the credential-rotation behavior is documented and tested\n59\t- The design documents how the BFF will consume the shared platform PostgreSQL connection string when it becomes available in RHOAI 3.6, and how a user points playground RAG at an external vector database\n60\t\n61\t## HLR Traceability\n62\t\n63\t- HLR-1 (P0 — default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning): this epic is the out-of-the-box delivery mechanism for the BFF-managed outcome\n64\t- HLR-2 (P0 — playground RAG workflows function with no loss of current capability): end-to-end workflow validation\n65\t- HLR-3 (P0 — vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance): the provisioned PostgreSQL runs outside the OGX process\n66\t- HLR-4 (P0 — multi-tenant scoping, per-namespace isolation): namespace-scoped provisioning\n67\t- HLR-5 (P1 — lightweight for resource-constrained environments): reference-based, benchmark-adjusted sizing\n68\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9073032f06d45f45","agent_id":"ab680fe0d56628d78","session_id":"02e875f2-6cfe-4933-bc28-36b9ef9ef3bb","uuid":"56d6a269-5977-452d-b359-340afbed90fe","timestamp":"2026-10-26T20:48:08.324Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8fb7b0111f4f5c4d","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
